WEBVTT

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.570
The battle between Akagi and Washizu.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.610
<b>Episode 19</b>

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.610
<b>The Confusion of the Demon</b>

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.150
The first match is coming to an end.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.560
Forced into a disadvantageous situation,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.560
Akagi tries to regain ground 
with his desperate bluff.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:40.640
Pon.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:42.280
Pon.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:44.550
Pon.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.210
<i>A honitsu?</i>

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:55.790
<i>Little punk...</i>

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.960
<i>Akagi's desperate bluff at 
the most crucial moment.</i>

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.970
<i>Now we have to see if it 
works on Washizu or not.</i>

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.470
<i>I see.</i>

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:15.810
<i>He was waiting for me to break up 
my closed wait on the character tiles.</i>

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.030
<i>He called three times after I broke it,</i>

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.980
<i>making the 6 of characters 
difficult to discard.</i>

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.480
<i>Don't think you've got me, though.</i>

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.990
<i>There's no doubt that the 
luck's with me this hand.</i>

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.130
<i>The winner will be me.</i>

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:41.830
<i>I'm in tenpai?</i>

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.200
<i>If this 6 of characters passes,</i>

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.910
<i>I'll be in tenpai, with 
a 2, 5, 8 of bamboo wait.</i>

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.170
<i>He has the 5 of bamboo, 
so I could win right away.</i>

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.720
<i>If this 6 of characters passes, I win.</i>

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.070
<i>Discard the 6 of characters and win.</i>

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.170
<i>Normally, that configuration 
after three calls</i>

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.530
<i>means that this 6 of 
characters is his target.</i>

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:11.900
<i>I would never be able to 
discard it, but here, I can.</i>

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.280
<i>Looking at it more broadly, 
the game's flow is currently on my side.</i>

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.830
<i>Meaning the tile I must discard</i>

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.910
<i>to get into tenpai can't 
be Akagi's winning tile.</i>

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.020
<i>His wait is probably...</i>

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:25.910
<i>a closed wait for the 8 of characters.</i>

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:28.910
<i>I will make it pass.</i>

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.920
<i>This 6 of characters will pass.</i>

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.600
<i>No, no. This is a bad habit of mine.</i>

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.940
<i>I always want to finish them off quickly.</i>

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:58.010
<i>But if I think about it, there's no 
reason for me to do him this favor.</i>

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.830
<i>I know he's waiting on 
the 8 of characters.</i>

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:02.450
<i>Or possibly, he's not in tenpai.</i>

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:05.120
<i>I don't think he will win 
from this 6 of characters,</i>

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:08.940
<i>and yet it's not 100% safe, either.</i>

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.720
<i>Which means there's no reason 
to push myself to discard it.</i>

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.500
<i>If I just take a moment 
to think about it,</i>

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:18.830
<i>it doesn't matter if he is waiting 
for the 8, or the 6 and 9 of characters.</i>

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:23.440
<i>There are very few of 
those tiles left anyway.</i>

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:28.300
<i>For starters, Suzuki has a 6, 
9 and three 8 of characters.</i>

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:33.540
<i>I also have a 6 of characters and 
I've discarded a 9 of characters.</i>

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.040
<i>And he has two 6 of characters.</i>

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.210
<i>Regardless of which tile he is waiting 
for, there's only one left of each.</i>

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.250
<i>Either the last 8 or 9 of characters.</i>

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:46.420
<i>That's it.</i>

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:48.790
<i>Which are grim chances.</i>

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:50.640
<i>There's no way he can 
draw them himself.</i>

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.820
<i>So his tenpai is effectively dead.</i>

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:54.870
<i>Even if the danger is low,</i>

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.800
<i>there's no reason for me to 
risk playing into his hand.</i>

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:03.540
<i>I can play around that tile 
and keep the game going.</i>

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.630
If anything, Akagi's lack 
of luck caused this.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:16.600
Whether it's for the 6, 
or the 8 and 9 of characters,

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.130
these waits are all almost dead.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:21.370
But that bad luck was actually fortunate.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.300
Akagi survived.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.300
He survived in order to survive.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:33.720
<i>What?</i>

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:37.100
<i>He switched out that tile 
and discarded a west wind?</i>

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.120
<i>So he wasn't in tenpai?</i>

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:40.310
<i>Damn it...</i>

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.760
He is slowly recovering.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.320
The game was mostly going 
in Washizu's favor,

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.060
but slowly, some of that luck 
is starting to return to Akagi.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:55.750
That was his aim from 
the very beginning.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:59.360
If Washizu and Akagi had both 
simply let their hands progress

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.260
in this fourth hand, Washizu 
would have most likely won first.

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:04.560
Luck was not on Akagi's side.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:10.360
To win, Akagi had to force Washizu 
to make a compromise somewhere.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.310
<i>What?</i>

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.810
The chance to win only comes after 
Washizu has made that compromise.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.050
<i>What the hell is he doing?</i>

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:28.390
<i>There's no doubt that he's been 
going for a honitsu up until now.</i>

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:30.370
<i>But since he discarded 
the 6 of characters</i>

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:33.890
<i>and kept the 3 of circles, 
that is no longer possible.</i>

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:35.750
<i>Which means...</i>

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:37.760
<i>a toitoi?</i>

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.930
<i>If that black tile is a 3 of circles,</i>

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:43.440
<i>he has a toitoi with east winds 
and red dragons, for a mangan.</i>

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.770
<i>The cop has a 3 of circles, too.</i>

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:49.290
<i>It's over.</i>

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:53.410
<i>If he's in tenpai, the other will 
play into his hand and it'll be over.</i>

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.290
<i>I guess he's not in tenpai.</i>

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:10.790
<i>Of course.</i>

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:12.810
<i>How could you be in tenpai?</i>

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.230
<i>You don't have the luck 
to get a toitoi right now.</i>

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:20.160
<i>Luck is on my side this hand.</i>

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.680
<i>I'll be in tenpai before 
you complete your toitoi.</i>

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:27.760
<i>That's right.</i>

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:29.500
<i>You can't get into tenpai that easily.</i>

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:33.680
<i>Did you think you'd get your hands 
on the last 3 of circles that easily?</i>

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:38.350
<i>While you're wasting time, 
I'll be making my comeback.</i>

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:40.850
<i>Yes, this will work.</i>

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.090
<i>Nice and easy.</i>

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:47.270
<i>Now I can have a three-way 
wait on the bamboo tiles.</i>

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:49.070
<i>An ideal formation.</i>

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:51.100
<i>I'll keep these.</i>

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:55.370
<i>The question is, do I get rid of 
the circles or the characters?</i>

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:56.830
<i>We're already on the 13th turn.</i>

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:59.110
<i>There aren't many draws left.</i>

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:03.470
<i>Then I should probably make 
it a 5, 8 of characters wait</i>

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:05.650
<i>so Suzuki can deal 
into my hand right away.</i>

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:06.820
<i>That's what I'll do.</i>

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:10.320
<i>I want to get rid of my circles...</i>

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:11.590
<i>Except...</i>

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:16.320
<i>He's an odd kid.</i>

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.770
<i>His game of Mahjong's basically 
on the verge of death,</i>

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:22.370
<i>yet it seems to be closely following mine.</i>

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:26.070
<i>It's positioned in a way that 
restricts my movements again.</i>

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:30.490
<i>He's most likely aiming for a toitoi,</i>

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:37.440
<i>but he could also have a 
2, 5 or 1, 4 of circles wait.</i>

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.220
<i>Since he already has a 1 
of circles in his discards,</i>

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.580
<i>he could have a closed 
wait on the 4 of circles.</i>

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:47.900
<i>At any rate, he could also win with 
just the east winds and red dragons.</i>

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:51.600
<i>For the dealer, a 40 fu, 
2 han hand is worth 3,900.</i>

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:55.100
<i>It's not like he can take the lead 
if that cop deals into his hand.</i>

00:08:55.100 --> 00:09:00.370
<i>He couldn't, even if he drew it himself.</i>

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:07.740
<i>He'll just get to stay dealer, 
but it won't overturn my 6,800 lead.</i>

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:11.160
<i>But not if I discard that tile.</i>

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.750
<i>If I deal into that 3,900 hand, 
it would be enough to put him on top.</i>

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:20.850
<i>So then, discarding any circle 
tiles would be reckless.</i>

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:22.790
<i>Wait.</i>

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.880
<i>Suzuki has a 5 of circles.</i>

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:30.860
<i>That means I can get Suzuki 
to test it out for me.</i>

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:35.110
<i>If he discards it and it passes, 
I can discard it, too.</i>

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:40.110
<i>He won't take over the lead 
if Suzuki deals into his hand.</i>

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:44.830
<i>We'd start a new match and it 
won't be much skin off my nose.</i>

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:47.610
<i>This is the best way.</i>

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.280
Washizu discards the 6 of circles 
and sends his underling a sign.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:56.620
Suzuki gets the message

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:02.190
and discards the highly 
risky tile, the 5 of circles.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:05.700
But Akagi doesn't move.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:12.610
<i>It went through.</i>

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:15.950
<i>So that means he's got a closed 
wait for the 4 of circles,</i>

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:17.940
<i>or is possibly not even in tenpai.</i>

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:21.900
<i>Either way, seeing that he 
can't win off my 5 of circles,</i>

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:24.050
<i>it's the end of the road for him.</i>

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:26.320
He draws an 8 of bamboo.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:29.310
Once Washizu discards the 5 of 
circles, he will be in tenpai.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:31.630
<i>I won!</i>

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:33.220
<i>I just have to discard this 5 of circles,</i>

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:36.300
<i>then Suzuki will deal the 5 of characters 
into my hand and it will be over.</i>

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:37.090
<i>Victory.</i>

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:40.010
<i>There were some twists and 
turns, but in the end,</i>

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:43.100
<i>this brat was also no match for me.</i>

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:48.340
<i>There is simply nobody 
who can defeat me.</i>

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:50.340
Ron.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:51.290
What?!

00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:58.760
Akagi!

00:10:58.760 --> 00:10:59.930
You did it!

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:01.600
You did it, Akagi!

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:03.000
East wind, red dragon.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:04.620
A 40 fu, 2 han hand, as dealer.

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:08.350
Old man, this 3,900 direct 
hit puts me back on top.

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:10.890
The first match is over.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:12.740
<i>It can't be!</i>

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:15.850
<i>How could he ignore Suzuki's 5 of circles?</i>

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:18.580
<i>Sure, getting it from me 
would put him back on top,</i>

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:20.920
<i>but he didn't have the luxury of waiting!</i>

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:24.080
<i>It's not such a big deal that he figured out</i>

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.410
<i>that I'd discard the 5 of circles.</i>

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:29.450
<i>With this game's clear tiles,</i>

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:32.410
<i>making such predictions isn't that difficult.</i>

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:34.310
<i>He may have predicted the sequence of</i>

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:36.420
<i>Suzuki discarding the 5 of 
circles after I discarded my 6</i>

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:40.620
<i>as paving the way for me to 
discard the 5 of circles again,</i>

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:44.860
<i>and ignored Suzuki's 5 
of circles based on that.</i>

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:50.710
<i>But that's something you could say 
only under normal circumstances.</i>

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:53.300
<i>This is different.</i>

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:57.020
<i>If I had won this hand, 
he would have died!</i>

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.170
<i>He would have lost his life!</i>

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:01.080
<i>That's not something you can just do.</i>

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:04.570
<i>Even if he suspected, no, 
even if he was 99% certain</i>

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:07.280
<i>that Suzuki dealt the 5 
of circles so that I could</i>

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:09.540
<i>safely discard it afterwards...</i>

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.950
<i>so long as that certainty isn't 100%,</i>

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:14.890
<i>so long as there remains even 
a 1% feeling of uncertainty,</i>

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:18.080
<i>there's no way that he 
could ignore the chance</i>

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.270
<i>to save his life with Suzuki's 5 of circles!</i>

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:25.610
<i>Does this kid not want to save himself?</i>

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:28.170
<i>Does he not want to keep living?</i>

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:30.000
<i>To see the next day?</i>

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:34.560
<i>You lunatic!</i>

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:42.630
The first match ended with Akagi's victory.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:45.860
Akagi receives the bonus payment 
for the direct hit off Washizu,

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:48.130
the 3.9 million yen.

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:49.710
Now the regular payments will be settled.

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:54.630
Akagi is in first place with 38,500.

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:58.230
Washizu is in second place with 37,500.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:04.460
<b>|</b> Hand |  #   <b>|</b>          Akagi            <b>|</b>       Washizu
<b>|</b>          Points           <b>|</b>           Points {these lines have been timed for the whole time that table is on the screen. Please adjust as necessary. Also, open the original TL in notepad to see the layout if you don't understand this -el}

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:06.030
East

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:07.830
First 
Match

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:01.340
Akagi receives a 20,000 bonus 
for being in first place,

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:06.030
South

00:13:01.340 --> 00:13:05.710
and a 30,000 ranking bonus, 
bringing him to a total of 88,500.

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:11.790
Payment

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.160
Washizu only receives 
the 10,000 ranking bonus

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:09.910
for being in second place,

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:11.780
bringing him to 47,500.

00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:14.970
The difference between 
the two is 41,000 points.

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:18.280
This is the base for the amount 
of money Akagi can receive.

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:21.550
Were he to exchange it all for 
money, it would be 41 million yen.

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:23.970
But that's not all.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:27.590
With the direct hit bonus of 3.9 million,

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:29.560
the total in bonus payments 
increases to 11.5 million yen.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:33.550
Meaning Akagi has taken 52.5 
million yen from Washizu

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:35.580
in this first East-South match.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:39.660
That would be about 
500 million yen today.

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:43.410
<i>He could win.</i>

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.330
<i>I don't think someone else could do it,</i>

00:13:45.330 --> 00:13:49.210
<i>but with Akagi, I'm starting to 
believe it's not impossible.</i>

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:52.600
<i>He just needs to survive 
six East-South matches.</i>

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:56.220
<i>If he does that, he can strip 
Washizu of his entire fortune.</i>

00:13:57.500 --> 00:14:02.190
The money Akagi is entitled to 
should correspond to 41,000 points.

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:04.400
41 million yen.

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:10.570
But Akagi has lost 600 CCs of blood 
in this East-South match.

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:13.810
So first, he must transfuse this blood

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:18.070
back into his body before 
the next match starts.

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:22.430
If not, should Washizu get a 
direct hit mangan off of him,

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:23.810
it would cost him 8,000 points.

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:25.690
800 CCs would be withdrawn.

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:28.980
Combined with the current 600 CCs, 
that would total 1,400 CCs.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:30.550
It might not be a lethal amount,

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:32.820
but it would be enough 
to muddle his thinking.

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:38.360
Keeping that in mind, it is essential 
that he get that blood back.

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:42.550
Accordingly, Washizu deducts 
600 CCs worth of money,

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:46.560
6 million yen, from the 41 million,

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:49.770
and hands Akagi the remaining 35 million.

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:53.560
Ougi has no objections.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:54.240
However...

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:55.840
That's not necessary.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:57.200
What?

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.580
I want all my winnings in cash.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.080
Akagi!

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:03.320
Akagi, don't be ridiculous!

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:04.530
<i>What?</i>

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:08.320
Akagi, come here!

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:12.380
What are you thinking? You'll die!

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:15.060
You'll die in a heartbeat if you 
underestimate Washizu like that.

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:20.430
A heartbeat from death 
is the right place to be.

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:26.900
If I were only a tiny push, 
a tiny step away from death,

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:29.940
that's the source of hope for Washizu.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:31.760
Ougi-san.

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:34.510
We can't strip Washizu of his hope.

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.550
Even as they get burned,

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:40.450
as long as there is hope, 
people will pursue it.

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:52.800
In the end, Akagi enters the next match 
without taking back a single drop of blood.

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:56.750
He starts off with a 600 CC deficit.

00:15:57.450 --> 00:16:01.810
Naturally, this means that he is in 
even more danger than in the first match.

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:04.690
Should he get hit 
directly with a mangan,

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:07.310
it would mean losing 
800 on top of the 600.

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:11.790
After having 1,400 CCs of 
blood taken from his body...

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:15.320
He would be on the verge of death.

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:19.760
<i>It's unthinkable.</i>

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:26.560
<i>To us it's unfathomable, but Akagi 
himself decided to do it this way.</i>

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:28.830
<i>That means it's probably necessary.</i>

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:33.670
<i>This genius's instinct and sense 
demand drastic measures.</i>

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:35.670
<i>So that he may win.</i>

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:40.410
<i>We can't understand his 
sense or calculations.</i>

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:42.810
<i>They're in another realm.</i>

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:46.200
<i>All we can do is pray.</i>

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:50.820
<i>All we can do is pray that this 
act doesn't blow up in his face.</i>

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:52.350
<i>Prayer is all we have.</i>

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:59.400
<i>To pray that the unjust and irrational 
nature of Mahjong won't show itself here.</i>

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:04.090
<i>That's how dangerous this game is.</i>

00:17:04.090 --> 00:17:06.810
<i>Here, strength does not 
always equal victory.</i>

00:17:06.810 --> 00:17:09.940
<i>Even the strongest are powerless 
against the fierce wind of pure luck,</i>

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:13.890
<i>and Akagi is no exception.</i>

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:18.310
<i>All we can do is pray that luck 
won't flow towards Washizu.</i>

00:17:18.830 --> 00:17:22.120
<i>If it's just moderate luck, 
Akagi can make it through.</i>

00:17:22.120 --> 00:17:23.500
<i>He should be fine.</i>

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:26.560
<i>So don't let Washizu get it!</i>

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:29.560
<i>That kind of fierce and uncontrollable luck!</i>

00:17:29.930 --> 00:17:35.040
Unfortunately, Ougi's fears 
were half on the mark.

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:41.200
A preemptive strike.

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:42.620
Tsumo.

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:46.710
Tanyao, tsumo, two dora. 
Mangan. 4,000; 2,000.

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:52.010
From the very beginning of the 
second hand, the luck is with Washizu.

00:17:52.510 --> 00:17:54.050
He draws his winning 
tile for a mangan hand.

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:56.320
He gains a 10,000 point lead.

00:17:57.350 --> 00:17:59.690
After that, he wins from

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:02.360
his underling dealing into his hand.

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:04.660
Then another tsumo win.

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:06.700
The East round is one-sided.

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:11.030
He has a 25,800 point lead on Akagi.

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:14.320
Then there are the bonus payments 
he receives from Akagi.

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:18.470
The two tsumo wins add up to 500 CCs.

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:22.340
Combined with the 600 CCs lost in the 
first game, that is a total of 1,100 CCs.

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:25.340
It is over half the lethal amount.

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:34.450
Even if he can avoid direct hits, it's 
useless if Washizu continues to be so lucky.

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:38.710
He won't get around 
having his blood drawn.

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:43.480
Were he to deal into a mangan 
hand, it would be 1,900 CCs.

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:45.060
Certain death.

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:46.800
It's a harsh situation.

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:48.570
The East round is rough.

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:52.440
As it is, Ougi's fears have 
been completely realized.

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:56.080
But the flow of the game changes 
once they move into the South round.

00:18:58.670 --> 00:18:59.850
South Hand 1.

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:03.430
Akagi unexpectedly gets 
a tanpin, sanshoku hand.

00:19:03.430 --> 00:19:05.850
It's an ideal big hitter.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:09.720
He also has two dora.

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:12.780
On top of that, many of his tiles are black,

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:14.220
making his waits difficult to read.

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:18.730
These black tiles prove 
to be very effective.

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:21.970
Ron.

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:25.240
Tanpin, sanshoku, two dora.

00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:26.470
Haneman.

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:31.440
In Washizu Mahjong, 
it's rare to see a direct hit

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:33.480
with a tanpin sanshoku hand.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:35.710
<i>Damn it.</i>

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:39.720
With this win, Akagi catches 
up to Washizu in one move.

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:42.950
Their gap has now narrowed 
to 1,800 points.

00:19:42.950 --> 00:19:46.290
It is also the first direct 
hit in this second match.

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:49.340
He gets a chance to transfuse 
some of his blood back.

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:53.200
This is big, as well.

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:58.090
With this 12,000 point win, 
he is now able to regain

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:01.970
all of the 1,100 CCs of 
blood he's lost until now.

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:08.250
He will be released from the fear 
of dealing into a mangan hand

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:11.440
and the instant death that would follow.

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:13.540
Or so it seemed.

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:15.250
I don't need it.

00:20:20.430 --> 00:20:23.050
Why, Akagi?!

00:20:23.050 --> 00:20:24.370
Why?

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:27.460
No matter how you slice it, 
you've got to get that blood back!

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:29.430
Akagi!

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:30.430
Money.

00:20:31.090 --> 00:20:33.070
I'm going to only grab 
money here like a miser.

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:38.070
Only then can I chase down Washizu.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:42.000
Only then can I...

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:43.950
kill Washizu.

00:20:52.730 --> 00:20:56.930
Akagi attacks with little 
regard for his own life.

00:20:56.930 --> 00:20:59.070
But will it be effective against Washizu?

00:21:00.200 --> 00:21:04.430
The second match of Washizu 
Mahjong has only just begun.

00:22:16.240 --> 00:22:19.150
Next time, "Hope and Folly."

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Next Episode</b>

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Hope and Folly</b>
